INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Does JavaScript support namespaces

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
JavaScript doesn’t support namespaces by default. So if you create any element (function, method, object, variable) then it becomes global and pollutes the global namespace. Let''s take an example of defining two functions without any namespace,

      ```javascript
      function func1() {
        console.log("This is a first definition");
      }
      function func1() {
        console.log("This is a second definition");
      }
      func1(); // This is a second definition
      ```

      It always calls the second function definition. In this case, namespaces will solve the name collision problem.

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

293.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "Does JavaScript support namespaces", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: How do you declare a namespace

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
Even though JavaScript lacks namespaces, we can use Objects, an IIFE (Immediately Invoked Function Expression) or `let`/`const` to create namespaces.

      1. **Using Object Literal Notation:** Let''s wrap variables and functions inside an Object literal which acts as a namespace. After that you can access them using object notation

         ```javascript
         var namespaceOne = {
             function func1() {
                 console.log("This is a first definition");
             }
         }
         var namespaceTwo = {
               function func1() {
                   console.log("This is a second definition");
               }
           }
         namespaceOne.func1(); // This is a first definition
         namespaceTwo.func1(); // This is a second definition
         ```

      2. **Using IIFE (Immediately invoked function expression):** The outer pair of parentheses of IIFE creates a local scope for all the code inside of it and makes the anonymous function a function expression. Due to that, you can create the same function in two different function expressions to act as a namespace.

         ```javascript
         (function () {
           function fun1() {
             console.log("This is a first definition");
           }
           fun1();
         })();

         (function () {
           function fun1() {
             console.log("This is a second definition");
           }
           fun1();
         })();
         ```

      3. **Using a block and a let/const declaration:** In ECMAScript 6, you can simply use a block and a let declaration to restrict the scope of a variable to a block.

         ```javascript
         {
           let myFunction = function fun1() {
             console.log("This is a first definition");
           };
           myFunction();
         }
         //myFunction(): ReferenceError: myFunction is not defined.

         {
           let myFunction = function fun1() {
             console.log("This is a second definition");
           };
           myFunction();
         }
         //myFunction(): ReferenceError: myFunction is not defined.
         ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

294.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "How do you declare a namespace", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
