INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Why do you need to avoid with statement

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
JavaScript''s with statement was intended to provide a shorthand for writing recurring accesses to objects. So it can help reduce file size by reducing the need to repeat a lengthy object reference without performance penalty. Let''s take an example where it is used to avoid redundancy when accessing an object several times.

      ```javascript
      a.b.c.greeting = "welcome";
      a.b.c.age = 32;
      ```

      Using `with` it turns this into:

      ```javascript
      with (a.b.c) {
        greeting = "welcome";
        age = 32;
      }
      ```

      But this `with` statement creates performance problems since one cannot predict whether an argument will refer to a real variable or to a property inside the with argument.

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

305.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "Why do you need to avoid with statement", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: What is the output of the following for loops

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
```javascript
      for (var i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        // global scope
        setTimeout(() => console.log(i));
      }

      for (let i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        // block scope
        setTimeout(() => console.log(i));
      }
      ```

      The output of the above for loops is 4 4 4 4 and 0 1 2 3

      **Explanation:** Due to the event queue/loop of javascript, the `setTimeout` callback function is called after the loop has been executed. Since the variable i is declared with the `var` keyword it became a global variable and the value was equal to 4 using iteration when the time `setTimeout` function is invoked. Hence, the output of the second loop is `4 4 4 4`.

      Whereas in the second loop, the variable i is declared as the `let` keyword it becomes a block scoped variable and it holds a new value(0, 1 ,2 3) for each iteration. Hence, the output of the first loop is `0 1 2 3`.

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

306.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "What is the output of the following for loops", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
