INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Can I redeclare let and const variables

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
No, you cannot redeclare let and const variables. If you do, it throws below error

      ```bash
      Uncaught SyntaxError: Identifier ''someVariable'' has already been declared
      ```

      **Explanation:** The variable declaration with `var` keyword refers to a function scope and the variable is treated as if it were declared at the top of the enclosing scope due to hoisting feature. So all the multiple declarations contributing to the same hoisted variable without any error. Let''s take an example of re-declaring variables in the same scope for both var and let/const variables.

      ```javascript
      var name = "John";
      function myFunc() {
        var name = "Nick";
        var name = "Abraham"; // Re-assigned in the same function block
        alert(name); // Abraham
      }
      myFunc();
      alert(name); // John
      ```

      The block-scoped multi-declaration throws syntax error,

      ```javascript
      let name = "John";
      function myFunc() {
        let name = "Nick";
        let name = "Abraham"; // Uncaught SyntaxError: Identifier ''name'' has already been declared
        alert(name);
      }

      myFunc();
      alert(name);
      ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

309.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "Can I redeclare let and const variables", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Does the `const` variable make the value immutable

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
No, the `const` variable doesn''t make the value immutable. But it disallows subsequent assignments(i.e, You can declare with assignment but can''t assign another value later)

      ```javascript
      const userList = [];
      userList.push("John"); // Can mutate even though it can''t re-assign
      console.log(userList); // [''John'']
      ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

