INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: What are default parameters

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
In ES5, we need to depend on logical OR operators to handle default values of function parameters. Whereas in ES6, Default function parameters feature allows parameters to be initialized with default values if no value or undefined is passed. Let''s compare the behavior with an examples,

      ```javascript
      //ES5
      var calculateArea = function (height, width) {
        height = height || 50;
        width = width || 60;

        return width * height;
      };
      console.log(calculateArea()); //300
      ```

      The default parameters makes the initialization more simpler,

      ```javascript
      //ES6
      var calculateArea = function (height = 50, width = 60) {
        return width * height;
      };

      console.log(calculateArea()); //300
      ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

311.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "What are default parameters", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: What are template literals

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
Template literals or template strings are string literals allowing embedded expressions. These are enclosed by the back-tick (`) character instead of double or single quotes.
      In ES6, this feature enables using dynamic expressions as below,

      ```javascript
      var greeting = `Welcome to JS World, Mr. ${firstName} ${lastName}.`;
      ```

      In ES5, you need break string like below,

      ```javascript
      var greeting = ''Welcome to JS World, Mr. '' + firstName + '' '' + lastName.`
      ```

      **Note:** You can use multi-line strings and string interpolation features with template literals.

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

312.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "What are template literals", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
