INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: What are the problems with postmessage target origin as wildcard

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
The second argument of postMessage method specifies which origin is allowed to receive the message. If you use the wildcard “\*” as an argument then any origin is allowed to receive the message. In this case, there is no way for the sender window to know if the target window is at the target origin when sending the message. If the target window has been navigated to another origin, the other origin would receive the data. Hence, this may lead to XSS vulnerabilities.

      ```javascript
      targetWindow.postMessage(message, "*");
      ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

329.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "What are the problems with postmessage target origin as wildcard", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: How do you avoid receiving postMessages from attackers

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
Since the listener listens for any message, an attacker can trick the application by sending a message from the attacker’s origin, which gives an impression that the receiver received the message from the actual sender’s window. You can avoid this issue by validating the origin of the message on the receiver''s end using the “message.origin” attribute.

      For example, let''s check the sender''s origin [http://www.some-sender.com](http://www.some-sender.com) on receiver side [www.some-receiver.com](www.some-receiver.com),

      ```javascript
      //Listener on http://www.some-receiver.com/
      window.addEventListener("message", function(message){
          if(/^http://www\.some-sender\.com$/.test(message.origin)){
               console.log(''You received the data from valid sender'', message.data);
         }
      });
      ```

      **[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

330.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "How do you avoid receiving postMessages from attackers", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
