INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: # 28. What is the output of below code

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
```javascript
function delay() {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 2000));
}

async function delayedLog(item) {
  await delay();
  console.log(item);
}

async function process(array) {
  array.forEach(async (item) => {
    await delayedLog(item);
  });
  console.log("Process completed!");
}
process([1, 2, 3, 5]);
```

- 1: 1 2 3 5 and Process completed!
- 2: 5 5 5 5 and Process completed!
- 3: Process completed! and 5 5 5 5
- 4: Process completed! and 1 2 3 5

<details><summary><b>Answer</b></summary>
<p>', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "JavaScript Core & DOM", "subtopic": "# 28. What is the output of below code", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/javascript-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: ## Answer: 4

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: JavaScript Core & DOM | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
The forEach method will not wait until all items are finished but it just runs the tasks and goes next. Hence, the last statement is displayed first followed by a sequence of promise resolutions.

But you control the array sequence using for..of loop,

```javascript
async function processArray(array) {
  for (const item of array) {
    await delayedLog(item);
  }
  console.log("Process completed!");
}
```

</p>
</details>

---

