INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: **Fiber Tree Structure**

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: React & Hooks | **Difficulty**: Medium

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
Each component in your app is represented by a **Fiber node** in a tree structure. A Fiber node contains:
      *   Component type
      *   Props & state
      *   Pointers to parent, child, and sibling nodes
      *   Effect tags to track changes (e.g., update, placement)
      *   This forms the **Fiber Tree**, a data structure React uses instead of the traditional call stack.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "React & Hooks", "subtopic": "**Fiber Tree Structure**", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Medium", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/reactjs-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: # **A. Render Phase (work-in-progress)**

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: React & Hooks | **Difficulty**: Easy

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
*   React builds a **work-in-progress Fiber tree**.
      *   It walks through each component (begin phase), calculates what needs to change, and collects side effects (complete phase).
      *   This phase is **interruptible**—React can pause it and resume later.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "React & Hooks", "subtopic": "# **A. Render Phase (work-in-progress)**", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Easy", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/reactjs-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
