INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: How do you solve performance corner cases while using context?

**Role**: Frontend Developer | **Topic**: React & Hooks | **Difficulty**: Hard

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
The context uses reference identity to determine when to re-render, there are some gotchas that could trigger unintentional renders in consumers when a provider’s parent re-renders.

    For example, the code below will re-render all consumers every time the Provider re-renders because a new object is always created for value.

    ```javascript
    class App extends React.Component {
      render() {
        return (
          <Provider value={{ something: "something" }}>
            <Toolbar />
          </Provider>
        );
      }
    }
    ```

    This can be solved by lifting up the value to parent state,

    ```javascript
    class App extends React.Component {
      constructor(props) {
        super(props);
        this.state = {
          value: { something: "something" },
        };
      }

      render() {
        return (
          <Provider value={this.state.value}>
            <Toolbar />
          </Provider>
        );
      }
    }
    ```

**[⬆ Back to Top](#table-of-contents)**

79.', '{"role": "Frontend Developer", "role_id": "frontend_developer", "topic": "React & Hooks", "subtopic": "How do you solve performance corner cases while using context?", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Hard", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sudheerj/reactjs-interview-questions/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
