INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Consistency patterns

**Role**: Backend Developer | **Topic**: Server Architecture & Scaling | **Difficulty**: Easy

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
With multiple copies of the same data, we are faced with options on how to synchronize them so clients have a consistent view of the data.  Recall the definition of consistency from the [CAP theorem](#cap-theorem) - Every read receives the most recent write or an error.', '{"role": "Backend Developer", "role_id": "backend_developer", "topic": "Server Architecture & Scaling", "subtopic": "Consistency patterns", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Easy", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Weak consistency

**Role**: Backend Developer | **Topic**: Server Architecture & Scaling | **Difficulty**: Medium

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
After a write, reads may or may not see it.  A best effort approach is taken.

This approach is seen in systems such as memcached.  Weak consistency works well in real time use cases such as VoIP, video chat, and realtime multiplayer games.  For example, if you are on a phone call and lose reception for a few seconds, when you regain connection you do not hear what was spoken during connection loss.', '{"role": "Backend Developer", "role_id": "backend_developer", "topic": "Server Architecture & Scaling", "subtopic": "Weak consistency", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Medium", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Eventual consistency

**Role**: Backend Developer | **Topic**: Server Architecture & Scaling | **Difficulty**: Easy

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
After a write, reads will eventually see it (typically within milliseconds).  Data is replicated asynchronously.

This approach is seen in systems such as DNS and email.  Eventual consistency works well in highly available systems.', '{"role": "Backend Developer", "role_id": "backend_developer", "topic": "Server Architecture & Scaling", "subtopic": "Eventual consistency", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Easy", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.document_embeddings (content, metadata)
VALUES ('### Technical Interview Question: Strong consistency

**Role**: Backend Developer | **Topic**: Server Architecture & Scaling | **Difficulty**: Easy

#### Technical Explanation & Model Answer
After a write, reads will see it.  Data is replicated synchronously.

This approach is seen in file systems and RDBMSes.  Strong consistency works well in systems that need transactions.', '{"role": "Backend Developer", "role_id": "backend_developer", "topic": "Server Architecture & Scaling", "subtopic": "Strong consistency", "category": "Theory", "difficulty": "Easy", "source": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer/master/README.md", "dataset_type": "Real-World-Curated-Online"}'::jsonb)
